<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="KidShell.SecurityHost" />

  <!--
    asInvoker, with the elevation requirement enforced in code instead.

    THE COMMENT THAT USED TO BE HERE WAS WRONG
    ------------------------------------------
    It said KidShell launched this binary with the "runas" verb, so the
    manifest did not need to ask. KidShell did not: the production client
    started it with UseShellExecute off and the standard streams redirected,
    which ignores a verb and cannot carry one. The security property the
    comment claimed therefore did not exist, and the helper refused every
    request on every child account. See BrokerTransportDefectTests.

    The manifest still says asInvoker, now for a reason that is actually
    true of this build:

      * the binary's normal mode is a Windows SERVICE. The service control
        manager starts it as LocalSystem, and requireAdministrator is
        meaningless to the SCM;
      * --self-test must run unelevated on a build agent, or the check that
        proves the broker rejects hostile input would be the one check
        nobody could run;
      * --approve-policy is launched WITH the runas verb, by
        ElevatedCommitApprovalChannel, which raises the prompt regardless of
        what the manifest says - and that path redirects nothing, so the
        verb is honoured. That is the one place in this product where a
        consent prompt belongs.

    Every mode that needs privilege checks for it at runtime and exits if it
    does not have it.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11. -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

</assembly>
